  C1  R_k(H o (x<->y)) = R_(g-k)(H) exactly            : True
      (H o sigma reverses the coefficient sequence A_k -> A_(g-k),
       i.e. r_i -> 1/r_i.  So the involution tau(P,Q)=(Q o sigma,
       P o sigma) -- a Jacobian pair with (n,m) swapped -- acts on the
       leading-form circuit as THM-3001's reversal.)
  C2  reciprocal-closed (complex mu) => R palindromic  : True  (40 multisets, degrees [2, 3, 4, 5, 6, 7])
  C3  palindromic => reciprocal-closed, exhaustive     : True  (103 palindromic of 1698 multisets, g=4,5)

==========================================================================
PART D -- the antipalindromic locus, coordinate-free
==========================================================================
  THM-3003 (extended in PART C): the circuit of H is antipalindromic
  <=> the direction multiset is invariant under t -> mu/t.  Every
  non-identity involution of P^1 is PGL_2-conjugate to such a map, and
  a linear change of (x,y) acts on directions by the full PGL_2.  So:
    'SOME coordinate system makes the leading-form circuit
     antipalindromic'  <=>  'the direction divisor has a nontrivial
     involutive PGL_2 symmetry'.

  K=2, mults (1,3)                       involutive symmetry: True   K=2: involution fixing both points, matrix [
  K=2, mults (2,2)                       involutive symmetry: True   K=2: involution fixing both points, matrix [
  K=3, mults (1,1,3)                     involutive symmetry: True   permutation (1, 0, 2), matrix [0, -1, -1, 0]
  K=3, mults (1,2,3)                     involutive symmetry: False  none
  K=4, mults (1,1,2,2) harmonic          involutive symmetry: True   permutation (0, 1, 3, 2), matrix [1, 0, 0, -
  K=4, mults (1,2,3,4)                   involutive symmetry: False  none
  K=5, mults (1,1,1,1,1) sym             involutive symmetry: True   permutation (2, 1, 0, 4, 3), matrix [1, -1, 

  PROVED: K>=3 with pairwise-DISTINCT multiplicities has NO nontrivial
  involutive symmetry (the only multiplicity-preserving involutive
  permutation is the identity, and a Mobius map fixing 3 points is the
  identity).  Hence for such H the leading-form circuit is
  antipalindromic in NO coordinate system whatsoever.

  exact control: H with K=3, mults [3, 2, 1], 103 random SL2(Z)
  coordinate changes -> palindromic circuits found: 0  (expected 0)
  mirror control: roots ['2', '1/2', '1', '1', '1'] (mults 1,1,3, K=3)
    R = ['121/115', '23/22', '23/22', '121/115']
    palindromic: True   (predicted True)

  census: fraction of REDUCED K-point direction divisors (all m_i=1)
  admitting a nontrivial involutive PGL_2 symmetry
  (= fraction for which SOME coordinate frame gives an antipalindromic
   leading-form circuit):
    K=3:  60/60 symmetric   (moduli dim 0; symmetric locus dim ~ 0)
    K=4:  60/60 symmetric   (moduli dim 1; symmetric locus dim ~ 1)
    K=5:   2/60 symmetric   (moduli dim 2; symmetric locus dim ~ 1)
    K=6:   5/60 symmetric   (moduli dim 3; symmetric locus dim ~ 2)

==========================================================================
PART E -- the exact ORDER LAW at each direction, extracted from L1
==========================================================================
  Write H = prod_i L_i^(m_i), e_i = ord_(L_i) P_(n-1), f_i = ord_(L_i) Q_(m-1).
  Lemma (exact):  ord_(L_i) Jac(F, H) = ord_(L_i)F + m_i - 1 + (extra),
  with extra >= 1 exactly when  ord_(L_i)(F) * g = m_i * deg F.
  Feeding this into L1 (Jac(P_n,Q_(m-1)) + Jac(P_(n-1),Q_m) = 0) gives

        e_i - f_i  =  m_i (a - b)  +  eps_i - delta_i            (J2-LAW)

  with delta_i = 0 unless e_i g = m_i (n-1), eps_i = 0 unless f_i g =
  m_i (m-1).  COROLLARY (n > m, both Jacobians nonzero): e_i >= 1 for
  every i, i.e. rad(H) divides P_(n-1) -- every direction at infinity
  reappears in the SUB-leading form of the higher-degree component.

   (n,m)=(  2,  2) a,b=(1,1) g= 2  e= 0 f= 0  d=0 eps=0  e-f=  0  law_rhs=  0  match=True
   (n,m)=(  6,  6) a,b=(1,1) g= 6  e= 4 f= 4  d=0 eps=0  e-f=  0  law_rhs=  0  match=True
   (n,m)=(  6,  2) a,b=(3,1) g= 2  e= 4 f= 0  d=0 eps=0  e-f=  4  law_rhs=  4  match=True
   (n,m)=(  4,  2) a,b=(2,1) g= 2  e= 2 f= 0  d=0 eps=0  e-f=  2  law_rhs=  2  match=True
   (n,m)=(  2,  2) a,b=(1,1) g= 2  e= 0 f= 0  d=0 eps=0  e-f=  0  law_rhs=  0  match=True
   (n,m)=(  4,  2) a,b=(2,1) g= 2  e= 2 f= 0  d=0 eps=0  e-f=  2  law_rhs=  2  match=True
   (n,m)=(  4,  4) a,b=(1,1) g= 4  e= 2 f= 2  d=0 eps=0  e-f=  0  law_rhs=  0  match=True
  (J2-LAW) exact, ALL instances                      : 81/81
  non-resonant instances with e-f = m_i(a-b)         : 81/81
  order lemma (extra>=1 <=> ord*g = m_i*deg)         : 162/162

  order lemma on forms with K >= 2 (the counterexample regime):
    exact instances (K in [2, 3, 4, 5, 6]) : 1004/1004

